  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAreas of smoke are pushing south-southwest across our area early \nthis morning with reduced visibilities now along and just to the \nsouth of the I-70 corridor. In developing light northeast flow, the \nHRRR continues to advect the smoke down to about the Ohio River \nthrough daybreak. Low level southwest flow will then begin to develop\nahead of a weak approaching mid level short wave. This will help to \npush the smoke back off to the northeast later this morning and into\nthis afternoon.\n\nThe smoke will make for a tricky temperature forecast today and have\ngenerally undercut guidance several degrees across about the \nnortheast half of our area, where the smoke should be the thickest \nand linger the longest. With a moist airmass in place, we will \ndestabilize through this afternoon, with the highest instability \nexpected across our southwest/west, where MLCapes of around 2000 \nJ/KG will be possible. As a result, scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will become more widespread across our west/southwest \nthrough this afternoon. These should then pivot east into eastern \nportions of our area later this afternoon into this evening, but may\nweaken/become more widely scattered in coverage if the smoke hangs \naround long enough to limit the instability in the northeast. \n\nPWs will be in excess of 2 inches today and with relatively light \nflow, locally heavy downpours/isolated flash flooding will be \npossible this afternoon into this evening, primarily for areas to \nthe west of I-75. With the potential for higher dcapes, it will also\nbe tough to rule out an isolated severe thunderstorm with damaging \nwind the main threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nA mid level trough axis pivoting down across the Great Lakes region \nSaturday will help push a cold front southeast across our area later\nSaturday afternoon into Saturday night. The airmass ahead of this \nwill be fairly unstable with MLCAPEs of around 2000 J/KG. In the low\nlevel convergence along and ahead of this front, a line of \nthunderstorms will likely drop south-southeast across our area in \nthe 20Z-02Z time frame Saturday afternoon into Saturday evening. \nThis will be accompanied by an axis of better deep/low level shear so\na few severe thunderstorms can be expected with damaging wind the \nmain threat.",
